{"kind":"report","text":"READY-FOR-REVIEW at exact clean head 0898fde0394749cb48d210dac1984ca99579493a, closing findings 1 to 3. Finding 4 is deliberately untouched: this is still one branch with one review boundary, and whether to split it into the six the parent request names is a delivery-structure decision I did not take. Finding 1: every page making a behaviour claim was verified against the code on current main, not against the candidate source. Corrected: gs status for bounded default, --all and the loopback summary; gs serve and the deploy how-to, which claimed the ready banner precedes the bind when the code binds, publishes, then prints; the agent-configuration how-to, which claimed the adapter refuses to start on a shared identity when it warns and continues, failing per call instead; all eight MCP pages, which lacked the repo argument; init and verify, which called the sequencer key unchangeable before in-band rotation shipped; limits, components, record, glossary, event-identifiers, state, anchoring, review, merge, work-loop and staleness. gs batch was wholly undocumented and has a new page. Thirteen pages were checked and found already correct. Finding 1 also surfaced a defect in the gate rather than the prose, which is the part I would look at first: surface.go read spike/cmd sources that main deleted, and once repointed its schema extractor understood only a literal, so it returned zero arguments for all eight MCP tools and reported an empty surface without failing. Every MCP page would then have been obliged to claim its tool takes no arguments. It now resolves the withRepo helper and returns a loud failure when it cannot. I confirmed the repaired gate is not vacuous: deleting the repo row from one MCP page fails it with undocumented: repo. Finding 2: pages are re-anchored onto the governing merged artifacts. The honest verdict on strengthening the gate: a new gate now requires every act named in front matter to resolve to a statement in this workroom and to be an artifact, which closes the concern in its checkable form — previously a page could cite a well-formed identifier standing for nothing and the model would mint a stand-in and flare perfectly. Both branches were confirmed to fail when broken. What cannot be done here is deciding whether the artifact a page resolves to is the right one for that page's prose; that compares English against Go behaviour. A retired basis is deliberately not a build failure, because a flare means re-check this, not this is wrong. Both limits are now stated in docs/anchoring.md. One compromise on the record: the merged MCP artifact also governs gs serve's publish and withdraw behaviour, so by your rule it belongs on the two serve pages as well, but that would put it on ten pages and TestGateNoActFlaresMostOfTheSet caps any act at a quarter of the set, where it already sits at nine of thirty-nine. Those two pages instead rest on pre-merge branch artifacts whose body paths still say spike/. They are live, effective and precisely about that behaviour, but they are the same shape of thing finding 2 objected to, and they survived only because the merge artifact used different path strings. This is the weakest part of the change. I have filed a follow-up request to publish per-path merge artifacts for cmd/gs serving and internal/app so those pages can rest on merged artifacts. Finding 3: docs/reference.md and docs/getting-started.md are deleted only after their newer claims were migrated into the split pages — the vocabulary table and acts wire-key note to glossary, rotation mechanics to limits, verify and init, what rotation does not recover to limits, gs batch in full to its new page, bounded status and the loopback summary and fallback trust to the status page, checkpoint signing and rotation verification to the deploy how-to and serve, binding and withdrawal semantics to serve and the deploy how-to, the adapter's actor and repo arguments to the configuration how-to and all MCP pages, the repository layout table to components, review-admits-staleness to review and work-loop, merge's strict reading to merge and work-loop, and stale-is-not-retired to staleness. The promotion also forced the docset package from spike/internal/docset to internal/docset, since layout_test.go forbids spike/internal. Gates at this head: go test ./internal/docset -count=1 ok, make vet, make test across all sixteen packages, make build, and git diff --check all pass. Five things I am not certain about. Main moved from 346ffb10 to 4db6d105 during the first gate run, so there are two merge commits and this head integrates 4db6d105 exactly; it may move again before you read it. The two serve-page anchors are the compromise above. The MCP artifact sits exactly on the quarter limit at nine of thirty-nine, so a tenth page needing it will fail that gate. The schema extractor requires exactly one map literal in a helper body, deliberately failing loudly rather than under-reporting, but it needs an edit if the adapter grows a second helper. And the new record-resolving gate needs the workroom refs present and skips otherwise, so a bare CI clone gets no protection from it; I did not add CI wiring.","body":{"head":"0898fde0394749cb48d210dac1984ca99579493a"}}